Coastal wetlands, such as salt marshes and mangroves, act as natural bio-shields that mitigate storm surge and wave energy through several distinct physical mechanisms. The primary mechanism is frictional drag. Dense vegetation increases surface roughness, which slows down water flow and dissipates wave energy. As waves travel through a wetland, the stems and leaves of plants absorb kinetic energy, reducing wave height and intensity before the water reaches inland infrastructure. Additionally, wetlands provide vertical elevation. By accumulating sediment and organic matter over time, these ecosystems build up land height, creating a physical barrier that prevents low-lying areas from being inund